WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.620
In the "Uncharted" movie, gamers have a lot
to recognize, Nathan Drake meets Nathan Drake,

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.540
and Sully gets his mustache… eventually.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.299
The first shot of "Uncharted" is a fade-in
on Tom Holland's Nathan Drake dangling upside

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:16.020
down from a cargo container flapping out the
back of an in-flight airplane.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.360
It's a bold way to kick off the movie, and
also a reference to two different games at

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.620
the same time.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.700
The shot itself, which shows Nate trying to
get his bearings in the middle of a dangerous

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.090
situation after briefly blacking out, harkens
back to the first scene of "Uncharted 2: Among

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.500
Thieves," in which Nate wakes up in a wrecked
train car hanging off the side of a cliff.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.371
In both cases, the action scene shown at the
start ends up being a flash-forward set in

00:00:36.371 --> 00:00:37.650
the middle of the actual story.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.010
That's a fun nod in itself, but the "Uncharted"
movie twists the reference by placing it in

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.170
a cargo plane — an allusion to a totally
different action sequence from "Uncharted

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.410
3: Drake's Deception."

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.410
Ah, crap

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.530
Every debonair action hero has to have at
least one recognizable catchphrase, and in

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.430
the case of Nathan Drake, it's just two words:

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.390
"Oh, crap."

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.410
While simple, this brief expletive is uttered
time and time again by Nate throughout the

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:03.410
"Uncharted" games.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.299
Ancient machinery breaking down and falling
on top of you?

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.299
Crap.

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:08.640
Just got double-crossed by your partner?

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.640
Crap.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.090
Need something to shout dramatically as you
leap from one crumbling ledge to another?

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.090
Crap.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.780
The phrase is just endlessly versatile.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.049
Holland's version of Drake gets to utter the
character's signature cry a couple different

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:22.670
times in the "Uncharted" film, and while it
might just seem like a throwaway line to those

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.579
who haven't played the games, it's a fun little
Easter egg for those who have.

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:31.009
Nathan Drake's origin story is eked out slowly
over the four mainline "Uncharted" games,

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:34.899
with many details not revealed until the events
of "Uncharted 4: A Thief's End."

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:38.999
A lot of those details show up in the movie
as well, albeit with a few changes.

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:42.969
As in the games, after a family tragedy, Nate
and Sam end up living in a Catholic orphanage

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.069
in Boston named for St. Francis.

00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:49.030
Sam, the older brother, ends up departing
the orphanage before Nate is old enough, leaving

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.139
him with a few mementos to remember him by.

00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:55.969
The first item Sam gives Nate is an old ring
with the Latin inscription "Sic Parvis Magna,"

00:01:55.969 --> 00:01:58.950
which translates to "greatness from small
beginnings."

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.799
This is Nate's most treasured keepsake in
the video games, though in the original version

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.119
of the story, he steals it from a museum rather
than getting it from Sam.

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:10.980
The movie's biggest blink-and-you'll-miss-it
Easter egg comes relatively early on, after

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.330
Sully first propositions Nate to help him
find the lost treasure of Ferdinand Magellan.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.040
Back at his own place, Nate digs through a
trunk full of mementos to find some old postcards

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.640
from his brother, and as he lifts the lid,
one of the faded stickers briefly spotted

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.090
by the camera is revealed to be the Naughty
Dog logo — the acclaimed game studio responsible

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.640
for creating "Uncharted."

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.720
Coincidentally, one of Naughty Dog's other
big gaming franchises, "The Last of Us," is

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.540
being adapted for a TV series at HBO starring
Pedro Pascal and Bella Ramsey.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.281
Given the cinematic nature of the developer's
games, we might be seeing the Naughty Dog

00:02:41.281 --> 00:02:44.760
logo in live action a lot more going forward.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.720
As proven by the opening cargo plane scene,
"Uncharted" has no qualms about ripping entire

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.290
sequences and story beats out of the video
games.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.480
Nate and Sully's auction heist is another
example of that.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.440
In "Uncharted 4," Nate, Sully, and Sam all
sneak into a secret black market auction in

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.260
Italy to steal — you guessed it — an old
cross that's actually the key to a lost treasure.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.270
The movie moves the auction to New York City
and ties its cross to Magellan, but the core

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.010
beats are still incredibly similar.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.100
"That's it up here.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.540
So we just have to climb up there and kill
the lights, grab the cross in the dark."

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.140
Specifically, the actual bidding scene is
basically pulled straight out of "Uncharted

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.140
4."

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.270
In the game, Nate goes gallivanting all over
the estate trying to figure out how to cut

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.270
the power.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:29.050
His delays force Sully to bid against the
game's main villain, Rafe Adler, to buy them

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:30.050
time.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.850
During the whole scene, Rafe is accompanied
by Nadine Ross — the leader of a mercenary

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.830
team who serves as his main muscle throughout
the game.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.560
The dynamic between Rafe and Nadine is strikingly
similar to that between Antonio Banderas'

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.560
Santiago Moncada and Tati Gabrielle's Jo Braddock
in the movie, both of whom are also present

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.310
and bid against Sully while Holland's Drake
tries to turn off the lights.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.040
Even the red tuxedo jacket Sully steals from
a waiter in the movie scene is pulled straight

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.840
from "Uncharted 4," though Sam is the one
to wear it there.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.950
"Uncharted" pays tribute to the franchise's
Hollywood inspiration a few different times.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:06.209
In Barcelona, for instance, Nate takes Sully
and Chloe to an old church where he believes

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:07.959
Magellan's gold to be buried.

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:11.709
When they enter the building, however, Nate
is shaken by the sight of a nun inside, flashing

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:14.630
him back to some less than happy memories
of his childhood.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.739
It's then that he drops the line, "Nuns.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:20.660
Why's it always have to be nuns?" which is
clearly a reference to Harrison Ford's famous

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.319
line from "Raiders of the Lost Ark."

00:04:22.319 --> 00:04:23.389
"Snakes.

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:30.180
Why did it have to be snakes?"

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.229
Shortly thereafter, in an attempt to bond
with Chloe, Nate asks her why she decided

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:35.680
to become Indiana Jones.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.730
This is obviously less of an Easter egg and
more of an overt reference that nearly breaks

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:40.730
the fourth wall.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:44.759
The revelation that Indiana Jones exists in
the "Uncharted" universe is funny and maybe

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:48.009
even a little odd, but you've got to give
the movie credit for not trying to hide the

00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:50.180
series' influences.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.430
Well before the theatrical release of "Uncharted,"
Nolan North — the man responsible for Nathan

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.659
Drake's voice and performance capture in the
video games — shared a picture of himself

00:04:57.659 --> 00:04:59.569
on set chatting with Tom Holland.

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:03.199
The pic immediately sparked speculation that
North could have a cameo in the film, which

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:05.270
ultimately ended up being true.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.680
Those in the know will recognize him as the
tourist lounging on the beach in the Philippines

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.330
who remarks to Nate and Chloe that he, too,
fell out of a plane once.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.740
Obviously, that line is referencing the events
of "Uncharted 3."

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.210
It's fun to see the two versions of Drake
interact, and if anything, the film maybe

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.159
could have benefitted from a little more Nolan
North.

00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:26.301
As a fun bonus, North's cameo is accompanied
by a quiet reprise of the "Uncharted" games'

00:05:26.301 --> 00:05:28.120
theme music.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.020
After nearly two hours of searching across
the globe, Nate finally finds Magellan's lost

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.819
gold aboard two pirate ships hidden in a cave.

00:05:34.819 --> 00:05:38.581
That particular setting is essentially the
same as the final act of "Uncharted 4," when

00:05:38.581 --> 00:05:43.360
Nate and his wife Elena discover Henry Avery's
lost ship hiding in a watery cavern on Turtle

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.360
Island.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.870
In both cases, the ships are loaded down with
treasure troves of gold, and they both end

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:51.139
up eluding Nate's grasp, albeit in different
ways.

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:55.280
In the game, Avery's ship gets set on fire
and quickly burns to ashes with the cave itself

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.949
collapsing on top of it.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:00.939
Nate and Rafe battle with pirate swords as
the ship implodes, with our hero just barely

00:06:00.939 --> 00:06:02.389
making it out alive.

00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:06.090
In the movie, Braddock hauls the two ships
out of the cave suspended from helicopters,

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.909
leading to an airborne battle in which Nate
once again employs the use of a rusty old

00:06:09.909 --> 00:06:10.909
sword.

00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:13.970
Though most of the treasure gets properly
claimed by the government of the Philippines

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:18.110
afterwards, Nate and Sully do get out with
a bit of gold for themselves — a small victory

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.380
that harkens back to a similar outcome in
the first "Uncharted" game.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.509
Like cartoon characters, video game characters
tend to wear the same outfits a lot.

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:30.009
As such, most famous characters like Nathan
Drake and the rest of the "Uncharted" gang

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:34.439
have pretty memorable looks, and many of those
are represented in the movie in one form or

00:06:34.439 --> 00:06:35.439
another.

00:06:35.439 --> 00:06:38.660
Holland's version of Nate sports several different
outfits throughout the film, and in the final

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.590
battle aboard the flying ships he assembles
a pretty spot-on replica of the character's

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:46.960
game look — khaki cargo pants, a dull white
Henley shirt with the sleeves pushed up, a

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.020
brown shoulder holster for his handgun, and
of course, Sir Francis Drake's ring on a string

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.400
around his neck.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.169
The outfit is very accurate to the games,
and it's a fun way to represent Nate fully

00:06:56.169 --> 00:06:58.690
becoming the treasure hunter fans know him
as.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:02.839
Later on, in the film's final post-credits
scene, Nate also wears a white and red baseball

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:06.360
shirt — another one of his classic looks
from the games.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.279
And he isn't the only one to get some Easter
egg costumes, either.

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:13.689
When Chloe first enters the story in Barcelona,
she's wearing a red outfit that's very reminiscent

00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:15.319
of her look from "Uncharted 2."

00:07:15.319 --> 00:07:17.900
"Oh, you know my brother Sam?

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.759
What else aren't you telling me?"

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:23.909
For most of "Uncharted," Sam Drake is merely
a specter — a missing man presumed to be

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:24.909
dead.

00:07:24.909 --> 00:07:28.099
However, in a scene tagged onto the end of
the movie, it's revealed that he's actually

00:07:28.099 --> 00:07:30.659
alive, wasting away in a foreign prison.

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:34.860
While the exact details of Sam's disappearance,
apparent death, and imprisonment are altered

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.430
a bit for the big screen, this is pretty much
exactly what happens to the character in the

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:39.430
video games.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:43.470
"Uncharted 4" shows how Sam and Nate sneak
into a Panamanian prison to get a vital clue

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.659
to a big treasure, only to be betrayed and
forced to flee.

00:07:46.659 --> 00:07:50.330
In the escape attempt, Sam is shot, and Nate
leaves him, believing him dead.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:53.979
Years later, it's revealed that Sam survived
the bullet, and that he was thrown in a dark

00:07:53.979 --> 00:07:54.979
cell to rot.

00:07:54.979 --> 00:07:59.339
If an "Uncharted 2" ever happens at Sony Pictures,
it should be interesting to see how the revelation

00:07:59.339 --> 00:08:01.849
of Sam's survival will work its way into the
plot.

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:05.740
He may have a bone or two to pick with Wahlberg's
Sully for leaving him behind on their quest

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.719
for Magellan's treasure, which could cause
some interesting tension.

00:08:09.719 --> 00:08:13.830
The movie's post-credits scene shows Nate
negotiating with an eyepatch-wearing man named

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.939
Gage, played by "Game of Thrones" alum Pilou
Asbæk.

00:08:16.939 --> 00:08:21.030
The two have agreed on a trade of Nate's ring
for an old Nazi map, but Gage quickly pulls

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.669
a double-cross and demands both.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:26.830
The most interesting part of the scene for
fans of the games is that Gage's boss is apparently

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:27.830
named Roman.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.651
A character named Gabriel Roman — an old
acquaintance of Sully's — is one of the

00:08:31.651 --> 00:08:33.580
main villains of the first "Uncharted" game.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.520
There's no way that's a coincidence, which
means that Roman is being set up as an antagonist

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.750
for the next movie, if one gets made.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:43.490
Additionally, the mention of a Nazi map is
also a reference to the first game in the

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.880
series, which involves a cursed treasure sought
by the Nazis during World War II.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.520
Perhaps the most exciting implication of these
references is that they pave the way for the

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.830
live-action debut of Elena Fisher, Nate's
constant companion and eventual life partner

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:56.900
in the games.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:00.540
In the canon of the games, Nate and Elena
meet at the beginning of "Uncharted: Drake's

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.081
Fortune," and it's hard to imagine another
movie being produced without Elena being a

00:09:04.081 --> 00:09:05.180
part of it.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:09.720
"Should've seen that one coming."

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.920
The "Uncharted" post-credit scene also includes
three fun Easter eggs for Sully — his mustache,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.210
his revolver, and a cigar.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.270
All three of those items are key parts of
Sully's video game persona, and they are glaringly

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:21.950
absent from most of the film.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:25.630
He's even wearing green in the shot, which
is arguably his most frequently worn color

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.200
throughout the franchise.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.100
It's fun to see Wahlberg's version of Sully
finally completed with all the necessary accessories,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.530
but the scene also raises the question — why
did it take him so long to get them?

